The rain had been falling for three days, and old Mr. Ellery had decided the world had simply run out of patience.

His daughter, Mara, came home soaked and carrying a small, battered suitcase. She had been away for a year, chasing a job she now described as "a disaster with a nice office." She stood in the doorway of the small house on the hill, shaking water from her hair, and said, "I'm not staying, Dad. I'm going back to the city."

Mr. Ellery nodded slowly, as though he had expected this for years. "Then go," he said. "The kettle's on."

Mara went upstairs. She unpacked a few things and made her bed, and then she sat on the edge of it and began to cry. She cried about the rent, the traffic, the man who had left her, and the fact that she had never once told her father how much she loved him. She cried until her voice gave out, and then she lay down on the bed and buried her face in the **pillow**, which smelled faintly of lavender and old paper.

She slept badly.

At dawn, Mr. Ellery climbed the stairs. He had brought her a cup of tea and a folded newspaper, and he had decided to say nothing at all. He had decided that the best thing a father could do was to be present, quietly, while his daughter worked through the wreckage of her own life.

He found her asleep, one arm thrown across her face, the other clutching the **pillow** as if it were the only solid thing in the world.

He sat on the edge of the bed and watched her breathe.

"You're exaggerating," he said softly.

Mara's eyes opened. "What?"

"The rain," he said. "You've been exaggerating the rain for three days. It's just rain."

Mara looked at him, confused. "It's been raining for three days, Dad."

"Yes," he said. "And it's been raining for a hundred years before that. And it will rain again tomorrow. But you're exaggerating it, and you're exaggerating the job, and you're exaggerating the man, and you're exaggerating the whole of it. That's what people do when they're afraid."

Mara sat up. "Afraid of what?"

"Of being ordinary," he said. "Of being here. Of being the person someone loves and doesn't have to perform for."

He reached over and took the **pillow** from her hands, setting it gently on the floor.

"I love you," he said. "Not because you're successful, or because you're kind, or because you're clever. I love you because you're mine. That's the whole of it. It's not a condition. It's not a reward. It's just... true."

Mara looked at him, and for the first time in a year, she stopped trying to be something. She stopped being the daughter who had to be right, or the daughter who had to be strong, or the daughter who had to be going somewhere. She was just a woman, tired and small, sitting on a bed in a house on a hill.

"I'm going back to the city," she said.

"Yes," he said. "You are. And I'll be here when you come back. And if you come back, I'll be here. And if you don't, I'll be here anyway."

Mara laughed, and then she cried again, and this time it wasn't the kind of crying that makes you feel smaller. It was the kind of crying that makes you feel held.

She lay back down, and this time she didn't bury her face in the **pillow**. She lay with her eyes open, looking at the ceiling, and she thought about how much she had been afraid of being loved.

Outside, the rain continued. It was still raining. But it was just rain.

Mara closed her eyes and slept, and for the first time in a year, she slept like a **cloud** — weightless, unbothered, and completely at rest.

When she woke, the rain had stopped. The light came in through the window, warm and ordinary and enough. She got up, made her bed, and went downstairs to find her father waiting with the kettle.

"Tea?" he asked.

"Yes," she said. "Tea."

And they drank it together, and neither of them exaggerated anything. [end of text]


